---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "kos protein powder",
      "kos protein powder reviews",
      "kos vegan protein powder",
      "kos protein powder recall",
      "kos organic plant protein",
      "is kos protein powder third party tested"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Plant-Based Picks",
    link: "/best-plant-based-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">KOS Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      A label-first look at KOS Organic Plant Protein: what the five-seed blend actually delivers, what KOS says about testing, heavy metals and recalls, and what it costs per 25 g of protein.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in It</a>
      <a href="#testing">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#heavy-metals">Heavy Metals and Prop 65</a>
      <a href="#recall">Recall History</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#reviews">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs-orgain">KOS vs Orgain</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          KOS protein powder is a USDA Organic plant blend that gives 20 g of protein and 160 calories per two-scoop serving, and KOS says every product is third-party tested for heavy metals. The catch is price and protein density: at about $2.45 per 25 g on Amazon, it costs more than Orgain. This review covers the label, testing, recall history, cost and buyer feedback.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. KOS Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            KOS Organic Plant Protein is a well-made, certified organic vegan powder with a clean sweetener list and a published heavy-metal testing program, but it is not a cheap or protein-dense way to hit your numbers. Each two-scoop serving gives 20 g of protein in 39 g of powder, per the label at the time of writing, and costs about $2.45 per 25 g of protein at the Amazon price we saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want USDA Organic, dairy-free and soy-free protein with no sucralose or erythritol, you like that KOS lets you look up a Certificate of Analysis by lot number, and you use protein as part of a smoothie or snack rather than as a pure muscle-building dose. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you are a tested athlete who needs NSF or Informed Sport certification, you watch sodium, or you want the most protein per dollar. In that case, look at the picks in our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">KOS Chocolate, per the label</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 2 scoops</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">160</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Added sugar</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$2.45</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g protein (Amazon)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. KOS Protein Powders and the Best Amazon Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three KOS tubs that are sold on Amazon, plus two organic plant proteins that cost less per gram. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw on November 16, 2026, and they move often.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in KOS Organic Plant Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            KOS Organic Plant Protein is a blend of five organic protein sources: pea protein, flaxseed, quinoa, pumpkin seed protein and chia seed. KOS confirms that list on its product FAQ. Around the protein sit organic coconut milk powder for creaminess, an organic superfood blend of fruit and vegetable powders, inulin and acacia fiber, a 12-nutrient vitamin and mineral blend, and a digestive enzyme blend. The chocolate version adds organic cocoa.
          </p>

          <p>
            The chocolate panel published on KOS.com reads as follows: a 39 g serving of two scoops, about 28 servings per 2.4 lb tub, 160 calories, 7 g of total fat with 4 g saturated, 330 mg of sodium, 9 g of carbohydrate, 3 g of fiber, 2 g of total sugar (all of it added) and 20 g of protein. It also lists 5 mg of iron (30% of the daily value), 1 mcg of vitamin B12 (40%), and a third or more of the daily value for vitamin E, selenium, copper, manganese and molybdenum. These figures match the KOS entry on our <a href="/protein-powder/kos-organic-plant-protein/">KOS Organic Plant Protein label page</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Label item</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">KOS Chocolate (per 39 g serving)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">160</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fat / saturated fat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7 g / 4 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbs / fiber / added sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">9 g / 3 g / 2 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sodium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">330 mg</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweeteners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Organic coconut sugar, stevia leaf extract, monk fruit extract</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Certifications (per KOS)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">USDA Organic (CCOF), Non-GMO Project Verified</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The sweeteners are organic coconut sugar, organic stevia leaf extract and organic monk fruit extract. KOS confirms the same trio for vanilla on its product page, and says there are no artificial sweeteners and no sugar alcohols such as erythritol. If you avoid stevia entirely, the unflavored and unsweetened tub is the only KOS protein that leaves all three sweeteners out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two label details deserve attention. First, protein density: 20 g of protein in 39 g of powder is about 51% protein by weight, because the coconut milk, superfoods and fiber take up space. A pure pea isolate runs closer to 80%. Second, 330 mg of sodium is 16% of the daily value in one shake, which is high for a protein powder and worth noting if you have high blood pressure or kidney disease. If either applies to you, check with your doctor or a registered dietitian before making this a daily habit.
          </p>

          <p>
            On flavors, KOS currently sells Chocolate, Vanilla, Chocolate Peanut Butter, Unflavored and Unsweetened, Salted Caramel Coffee, Strawberries and Cream, and Blueberry Muffin on its own store. The Salted Caramel Coffee version adds 50 mg of caffeine per serving, according to its Amazon listing. Sizes range from single-serve packets to 10, 15 and 28 serving tubs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is KOS Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, KOS says every product it sells is third-party tested for purity, potency and safety, but that testing is the brand's own program rather than a sport certification. On its <a href="https://kos.com/pages/quality-and-safety" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Quality and Safety page</a>, KOS states that raw materials and finished products are tested at independent, ISO 17025-accredited laboratories for heavy metals (lead, arsenic, cadmium and mercury), microbial contaminants and pesticide residues, as applicable.
          </p>

          <p>
            What makes the KOS claim more useful than most is the Certificate of Analysis lookup. The same page has a tool where you enter the lot number printed on the bottom or side of your tub and the last six digits of the UPC, and it returns the COA for that specific batch. That is a real step beyond brands that simply print the words third-party tested on the label and leave it there. We did not have a physical tub to enter a lot number, so we have not seen a KOS result ourselves and cannot report specific heavy-metal numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            What KOS does not have is an independent certification seal. A search of the NSF Certified for Sport registry for KOS returned no results when we checked, and KOS does not claim Informed Sport or Informed Choice. The difference matters for one group in particular: athletes subject to drug testing. Sport certifications screen every lot for banned substances under a certifier's rules. A brand-run lab program can be rigorous, but it is the brand deciding what to test and what counts as a pass.
          </p>

          <p>
            KOS also says its products are made in cGMP-audited, FDA-registered facilities, and lists its certifications as USDA Organic through CCOF and Non-GMO Project Verified for the plant protein powders. Kosher certification through cRc applies only to certain superfood blends and single-ingredient powders, per KOS, so do not assume the protein is kosher certified. If you need a certified powder, our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF certified protein powder</a> guide lists the options.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE DISTINCTION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Brand testing is not the same as certification.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">KOS publishes batch COAs from accredited labs, which is good practice. NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport add an outside certifier with its own pass rules. Casual users are usually fine with the first; drug-tested athletes need the second.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="heavy-metals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Does KOS Protein Powder Have Heavy Metals or a Prop 65 Warning?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Like almost every plant protein, KOS can contain trace heavy metals, and KOS says so itself. Its Quality and Safety FAQ explains that plants absorb minerals from soil, including trace amounts of lead, arsenic, cadmium and mercury, and that this is true of organic and conventional crops alike. Organic certification governs pesticides and farming practice; it does not guarantee low heavy-metal levels.
          </p>

          <p>
            KOS also addresses California Proposition 65 directly. The brand says that some KOS products carry a Prop 65 warning for California consumers where required, because plant ingredients and foods such as chocolate can naturally contain listed chemicals. A Prop 65 warning means a product could expose a consumer above California's very low safe-harbor level, which is far stricter than federal limits. It does not, on its own, mean the product is unsafe or unlawful, a point KOS also makes. KOS did not say which specific products carry the warning, so check the tub or the retailer listing for the version you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            What we could not find is independent, published heavy-metal data on KOS from a third party such as the Clean Label Project or a consumer testing group, so we are not going to rank KOS against other brands on metals. The honest summary is this: KOS tests for the four main heavy metals, gives you a way to see your batch's results, and acknowledges Prop 65 warnings on some products. That transparency is better than average for the category.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chocolate flavors in particular tend to carry more lead and cadmium than vanilla or unflavored versions, because cocoa itself is a known source. If heavy metals are your main worry, the unflavored KOS tub or a vanilla is the lower-risk choice within the line, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide explains how to read a COA. Pregnant or nursing readers should talk to a doctor before using any plant protein daily; KOS gives the same advice on its own safety page. For the wider picture, see <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recall" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Has KOS Protein Powder Been Recalled?
          </h2>

          <p>
            We found no recall notice for KOS protein powder as of November 16, 2026. A search of the <a href="https://www.fda.gov/safety/recalls-market-withdrawals-safety-alerts" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">FDA recalls, market withdrawals and safety alerts</a> database for KOS returned no KOS products, only unrelated matches for the word kosher, and the openFDA food enforcement records returned no matches for KOS as a recalling firm or in a product description.
          </p>

          <p>
            The search volume for KOS protein powder recall seems to come from two places. One is general worry about heavy metals in plant protein, which periodically makes headlines when a consumer group publishes tests. The other is confusion over Prop 65 warnings, which some shoppers read as a recall or a ban. Neither is a recall. A recall is a removal of product from the market, usually announced by the company and posted by the FDA.
          </p>

          <p>
            KOS's own FAQ answers the recall question vaguely. It says the company maintains quality procedures for product review and corrective action, and asks customers to contact product support for the most current information. That is not an admission of a recall, but it is not a flat no either, so if you have a specific tub in hand, the most reliable check is to search the FDA recall database for KOS on the day you read this, and to use the KOS COA lookup with your lot number.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a recall is announced in the future, it will appear on the FDA page linked above and usually on the brand's site. We will update this review if that happens. For now, the verified answer is that no KOS protein powder recall appears in the FDA records we searched.
          </p>
        </div>

