            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Third-Party Tested as a Certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                KOS says its batches are tested at accredited labs, and the COA lookup is genuinely useful. But that is a brand program, not NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport. If you compete in a tested sport, a brand testing claim will not protect you the way a registry-listed certification does.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Organic Means Free of Heavy Metals</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                USDA Organic rules cover pesticides and farming methods, not lead or cadmium in the soil. KOS itself says organic and conventional crops both absorb trace metals. Check your batch COA before assuming anything, and favor vanilla or unflavored over chocolate if heavy metals are your main concern.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Scoops Instead of Cost per 25 g</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The KOS serving is two scoops and 39 g of powder, and it holds 20 g of protein. Comparing tub prices or scoop counts with a 30 g protein powder hides the real gap. Divide the price by the total protein in the tub to compare fairly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Small Tubs Every Time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 10-serving tub and single packets are fine for testing a flavor, but they cost about $2.75 and nearly $5 per 25 g respectively. Once you know which flavor you like, the 28-serving tub on Amazon cut the cost to about $2.45 the day we checked.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Sodium Line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At 330 mg per serving, two KOS shakes a day add 660 mg of sodium, about 29% of the daily value. That is easy to miss in a protein powder. People with high blood pressure or kidney disease should account for it or ask their doctor first.
              </p>
            </div>
